      <p class="eyebrow">business-api-context</p>
      <h1>Business API map</h1>
      <p class="muted">How the Bunkai TMS API powers the product: auth tiers, critical journeys as call chains, the architecture behind /api/v1 and its external integrations.</p>
      <section id="overview" data-sources="openapi:*,route:app/api/v1/,route:lib/api/" data-updated="2026-10-05">
        <h2>What the API lets the business do</h2>
        <p>Bunkai TMS exposes one versioned REST surface, <code>/api/v1</code>, that the web app, scripts, CI/CD and AI agents all share. A team signs in, sets up a workspace and project, writes acceptance criteria and ATCs (reusable test steps with assertions), chains ATCs into Tests, runs them manually, files bugs from failed steps, and reads coverage and traceability reports.</p>
        <p>Every human action in the UI has the same call underneath, so a tester can do the whole loop from a terminal with a personal access token (PAT). The spec at <code>https://staging-upexbunkai.vercel.app/api/openapi</code> describes 102 operations in 27 tags (measured 2026-10-05 on staging); a May 2026 snapshot of this map counted 40.</p>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Tag group</th><th>Operations</th><th>What it covers</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Auth, Identity, Tokens</td><td>11</td><td>sign-up, sign-in, magic link, current user, PAT issue/revoke</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Workspaces, Projects, Invites, Billing</td><td>23</td><td>tenancy, membership, seats, Community to Cloud upgrade, export, deletion</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Modules, User Stories, Acceptance Criteria, Milestones, Environments</td><td>20</td><td>the requirement tree and where tests run</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ATCs, Tests, Test Plans</td><td>18</td><td>authoring and grouping of executable cases</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Runs, Bugs</td><td>14</td><td>manual execution and defect filing</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Coverage, Traceability, Metrics, Activity, Search, Notifications, Imports, Admin, Health</td><td>16</td><td>reporting, notifications, Jira import, cron digest, liveness</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p class="muted">Counts are per tag from the spec; the endpoint-by-endpoint contract lives in the OpenAPI document and the generated types in <code>api/schemas/</code>, never in this map.</p>
        
      </section>
      <section id="auth-model" data-sources="openapi:Auth,openapi:Tokens,openapi:Identity,route:lib/api/principal.ts,route:lib/api/capabilities.ts,route:lib/api/pat.ts,route:middleware.ts" data-updated="2026-10-05">
        <h2>Permission and auth model</h2>
        <p>Two credentials reach the API and both collapse into one <code>Principal</code> inside <code>resolveIdentity</code>: a Supabase session cookie (browser) or a Bearer PAT <code>bk_pat_&lt;12-char-prefix&gt;.&lt;secret&gt;</code>. A Bearer header wins over a stale cookie. Handlers then read and write through an RLS-scoped client acting as that user, so Postgres row-level security stays the single source of authorization; handlers do not re-check access in code.</p>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Who</th><th>How to acquire</th><th>Where enforced</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Public</td><td>anyone</td><td>no credential: <code>/auth/*</code> (6), <code>/health</code>, <code>GET /api/v1</code></td><td>route posture <code>public</code> in <code>lib/api/handler.ts</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Authenticated</td><td>any signed-in user</td><td>cookie or PAT: <code>/me</code>, <code>/tokens</code> (list), <code>/notifications</code>, <code>POST /workspaces</code>, <code>/invites/accept</code></td><td>posture <code>authenticated</code> (no capability)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Capability <code>atc:read</code> / <code>atc:write</code></td><td>PAT with that scope, or any cookie session</td><td><code>POST /auth/signin</code> returns a default PAT with <code>atc:read</code>, <code>atc:write</code>, <code>run:execute</code></td><td>posture <code>required:&lt;cap&gt;</code>; 34 read routes, 29 write routes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Capability <code>run:execute</code></td><td>same</td><td>same default PAT</td><td>4 routes: start, abort, finish, mark step</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Capability <code>workspace:admin</code></td><td>PAT minted for one workspace by an admin or owner</td><td><code>POST /tokens</code> with <code>workspace_id</code> (cookie session only)</td><td>7 routes: invites, billing checkout, <code>PATCH /workspaces/{id}</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cookie-only</td><td>browser session, never a PAT</td><td>web login (magic link or password)</td><td>9 routes: mint/revoke tokens, switch active workspace, data export, delete or restore workspace, leave workspace</td></tr>
          <tr><td>System secret</td><td>scheduler</td><td><code>CRON_SECRET</code> bearer, never issued to users</td><td><code>/admin/send-digest</code> (posture <code>public</code>, secret checked inside)</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p>A cookie session implicitly holds all four capabilities. Workspace roles (viewer, member, admin, owner) are enforced by RLS, so a PAT with <code>atc:write</code> still fails on a workspace where the user is only a viewer. A PAT cannot mint another PAT. Headless sign-in cannot request <code>workspace:admin</code> (ADR-0005 in the target repo).</p>
        <figure data-diagram="sequence" data-for="auth-model">
<svg role="img" aria-labelledby="api-seq-title api-seq-desc" viewBox="0 0 800 360" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
