      </section>
      <section id="journey-headless-auth" data-sources="openapi:Auth,openapi:Tokens,route:app/api/v1/auth/signin/route.ts" data-updated="2026-10-05">
        <h2>Journey: headless sign-in and token</h2>
        <p>Purpose: let scripts and agents act as a user without a browser. This is how this QA framework authenticates (<code>bun run api:login</code>).</p>
        <ol>
          <li><code>POST /api/v1/auth/signup</code> (202) sends a 6-digit email code; <code>POST /auth/confirm</code> finishes it. Passwords need 8+ characters here.</li>
          <li><code>POST /api/v1/auth/signin</code> with email and password verifies against Supabase Auth, sets session cookies and mints a PAT in the same call. Sign-in still accepts 6+ characters so older accounts keep working (BK-166).</li>
          <li>The caller sends <code>Authorization: Bearer bk_pat_...</code> from then on. The secret is shown once and stored only as a hash.</li>
          <li>A longer-lived or admin token needs a browser session: <code>POST /api/v1/tokens</code> is cookie-only.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Endpoints:</strong> <code>POST /auth/signup</code>, <code>/auth/confirm</code>, <code>/auth/signin</code>, <code>/auth/magic-link</code>, <code>/auth/check-email</code>, <code>/auth/resend</code>, <code>POST /tokens</code>, <code>GET /me</code>. <strong>Entities:</strong> <code>business-data-context</code> section <code>entity-access-tokens</code>. <strong>Features:</strong> <code>business-e2e-context</code> section <code>journey-first-setup</code> and <code>journey-issue-token</code>.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="journey-author-atc" data-sources="openapi:Modules,openapi:User Stories,openapi:Acceptance Criteria,openapi:ATCs,route:app/api/v1/atcs/route.ts" data-updated="2026-10-05" data-migrated-from=".context/business/business-api-map.md">
        <h2>Journey: author an ATC</h2>
        <p>Purpose: turn a requirement into a reusable, traceable test building block. Every ATC must link to one user story and at least one acceptance criterion of that story, which is how traceability is enforced.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><code>POST /workspaces/{id}/projects</code>, then <code>POST /projects/{id}/modules</code> builds the tree.</li>
          <li><code>POST /modules/{id}/user-stories</code>, then <code>POST /user-stories/{id}/acceptance-criteria</code> (or <code>POST /imports</code> to pull stories from Jira).</li>
          <li><code>POST /atcs</code> (201) with <code>title</code>, <code>module_id</code>, <code>user_story_id</code>, <code>acceptance_criterion_ids</code>, <code>layer</code> and <code>steps</code>; optional <code>technique</code>, <code>priority</code>, <code>tags</code>, <code>assertions</code>.</li>
          <li>Edit with <code>PATCH /atcs/{id}</code>, reuse with <code>POST /atcs/{id}/duplicate</code>, find with <code>GET /atcs/search</code>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Domain errors a client can branch on: <code>ac_outside_user_story</code>, <code>module_outside_project_subtree</code>, <code>steps_position_invalid</code>, <code>slug_collision</code>. <strong>Capability:</strong> <code>atc:write</code>. <strong>Entities:</strong> <code>entity-atc</code>, <code>entity-user-story</code>, <code>entity-acceptance-criterion</code>, <code>entity-module</code>. <strong>Features:</strong> <code>journey-author-atc</code>.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="journey-run-test" data-sources="openapi:Tests,openapi:Runs,route:app/api/v1/runs/route.ts,route:app/api/v1/tests/route.ts" data-updated="2026-10-05">
        <h2>Journey: compose a Test and run it</h2>
        <p>Purpose: execute a chain of ATCs by hand and record a verdict. This is the core value loop.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><code>POST /tests</code> with <code>title</code> and <code>atc_ids</code> chains ATCs into a Test. <code>Idempotency-Key</code> header is required. An empty chain returns <code>chain_empty</code>; <code>PATCH /tests/{id}/reorder</code> returns <code>chain_mismatch</code> or <code>chain_invalid</code> when the submitted order is not the exact step set.</li>
          <li><code>POST /projects/{id}/environments</code> defines where tests run (a Run needs one).</li>
          <li><code>POST /runs</code> with <code>test_id</code> and <code>environment_id</code> starts a Run (<code>Idempotency-Key</code> required; the same Test and token inside 24 hours returns the existing Run with 200). The RPC snapshots the chain into run steps. Errors: <code>no_executable_steps</code>, <code>environment_invalid</code>.</li>
          <li><code>POST /runs/{id}/steps/{stepId}/mark</code> sets passed, failed or blocked, with note and evidence URL.</li>
          <li><code>POST /runs/{id}/finish</code> closes it with a verdict; <code>POST /runs/{id}/abort</code> cancels.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Capability:</strong> <code>atc:write</code> for tests, <code>run:execute</code> for runs. <strong>Entities:</strong> <code>entity-test</code>, <code>entity-run</code>, <code>state-run</code>. <strong>Features:</strong> <code>journey-execute-run</code>.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="journey-report-bug" data-sources="openapi:Bugs,route:app/api/v1/bugs/,route:lib/bugs/constants.ts" data-updated="2026-10-05">
        <h2>Journey: file and move a bug</h2>
        <p>Purpose: turn a failed step into a tracked defect without leaving the run.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>A step is marked failed in a Run.</li>
          <li><code>POST /bugs</code> (201) files a bug linked to that failed step, or standalone.</li>
          <li><code>POST /bugs/{id}/assign</code> sets the owner.</li>
          <li><code>POST /bugs/{id}/status</code> advances one stage at a time: open, in_progress, resolved, closed. Skipping a stage is rejected.</li>
          <li>Read with <code>GET /projects/{id}/bugs</code>, <code>GET /projects/{id}/bugs/heatmap</code> and <code>GET /workspaces/{id}/open-bugs</code>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Entities:</strong> <code>entity-bug</code>, <code>state-bug</code>. <strong>Features:</strong> <code>journey-report-bug</code>.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="journey-upgrade-billing" data-sources="openapi:Billing,openapi:Workspaces,route:app/api/v1/billing/webhook/route.ts,route:lib/billing/" data-updated="2026-10-05">
        <h2>Journey: upgrade a workspace to Cloud</h2>
        <p>Purpose: the revenue path. An owner moves a workspace from Community to the paid Cloud plan by seat count.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><code>GET /workspaces/{id}/billing</code> shows plan and seat overview.</li>
          <li><code>POST /workspaces/{id}/billing/checkout</code> with <code>seat_quantity</code> (<code>workspace:admin</code>, <code>Idempotency-Key</code> required) creates a Stripe Checkout session and returns its URL. Only one open session per workspace; a second call returns 409. Stripe not configured returns 503.</li>
          <li>The buyer pays on Stripe. Stripe calls <code>POST /api/v1/billing/webhook</code>; the only authentication is the Stripe signature over the raw body.</li>
          <li>On <code>checkout.session.completed</code> or <code>async_payment_succeeded</code> the plan is applied, but only when Stripe says <code>payment_status = paid</code>. Failed or expired events release the session lock. An unknown session answers 5xx on purpose so Stripe retries.</li>
          <li><code>POST .../billing/checkout/cancel</code> abandons an open session.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Entities:</strong> <code>entity-workspace</code>, <code>entity-billing-checkout-session</code>. <strong>Features:</strong> <code>journey-billing-upgrade</code>.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="journey-invite-member" data-sources="openapi:Invites,route:app/api/v1/workspaces/,route:lib/api/invite-tokens.ts" data-updated="2026-10-05">
        <h2>Journey: invite a teammate</h2>
        <p>Purpose: grow a workspace's membership safely.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><code>POST /workspaces/{id}/invites</code> with <code>email</code> and optional <code>role</code> (<code>workspace:admin</code>, 201). Only the SHA-256 of the invite token is stored.</li>
          <li>The invitee receives the link by email and signs in.</li>
          <li><code>POST /invites/accept</code> with <code>token</code> redeems it (404 unknown, 409 already used).</li>
          <li>Admins list, resend or revoke with <code>GET</code>, <code>POST</code>, <code>DELETE /workspaces/{id}/invites/{inviteId}</code>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Entities:</strong> <code>entity-workspace-invite</code>, <code>entity-workspace-member</code>. <strong>Features:</strong> <code>journey-invite-teammate</code>.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="architecture" data-sources="route:app/api/v1/,route:lib/api/handler.ts,route:lib/supabase/,route:vercel.json,route:middleware.ts" data-updated="2026-10-05" data-migrated-from=".context/business/business-api-map.md">
        <h2>Architecture behind the API</h2>
        <p>One Next.js 15 app on Vercel serves both the UI and the API. Every <code>app/api/v1/**/route.ts</code> is wrapped by <code>withApiHandler</code>, which resolves the principal, checks the declared capability posture, and maps errors to one envelope. Business rules that must be atomic (run start, search, billing apply, deletion) live in SECURITY DEFINER Postgres RPCs, so the handler parses and maps while the database holds the rulebook.</p>
        <figure data-diagram="architecture" data-for="architecture">
<svg role="img" aria-labelledby="api-arch-title api-arch-desc" viewBox="0 0 920 420" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
<title id="api-arch-title">Architecture behind the API</title>
<desc id="api-arch-desc">Clients call route handlers on Vercel, which resolve a principal, verify with Supabase Auth, read and write Postgres under row-level security, and reach Stripe and Resend; a cron triggers the digest route.</desc>
